description:
Commissioners sit on committees that sometimes require out-of-County travel (for example, Treasure Coast Regional Planning Council, Florida Association of Counties, and Treasure Coast Sports Commission).
Commissioners, County Officers, and Staff may need to travel out-of-County for the following purposes: To attend legislative conferences, policy conferences, continuing education and meetings scheduled by the Florida Association of Counties, National Association of Counties, and local/regional legislative programs;
To meet with State Representatives, State Senators, and State Agencies such as the Departments of Environmental Protection and Transportation, among others;
To attend the National Hurricane Conference, Florida Governor's Hurricane Conference, Florida Shore & Beach Preservation Association Conference, the Florida Governor's Conference on Tourism, and other state or regional conferences directly related to committees on which Commissioners and/or County Staff members serve; and
To lobby on behalf of the County's various projects (and associated grants), legislative priorities, or other proposed legislation.

Pursuant to the County Attorney’s Employment Agreement, Section 6, the “County shall pay for all Attorney’s continuing legal education, as necessary to maintain membership in The Florida Bar.” The term “continuing legal education” includes program fees, travel, lodging, and per diem expenses. In addition, the County pays the County Attorney’s Florida Bar dues including dues related to Board certification, relevant Florida Bar section fees, Florida Association of County Attorneys (FACA) dues, and Indian River County Bar Association dues. Currently, the County Attorney serves as a member of the Board of Directors of FACA.
In addition to travel for continuing legal education, the County Attorney may need to travel out-of-county for the following purposes:
1. Legislative Conferences, policy conferences, FACA Board meetings and/or other meetings scheduled by the FACA and other national/regional/local government associations;
2. Meeting with State Representatives and/or and state agencies regarding County legal issues, programs and/or legislative issues;
3. Representing the County in litigation matters and hearings.

Pursuant to the County Administrator's Employment Agreement, Section 6 (Additional Benefits), the “...County shall pay for all professional dues, continuing education, subscriptions, and certification fees for County Administrator. Memberships and certifications will include ICMA (Credentialed Manager), Florida Association of County Managers, Florida City County Management Association..."
In addition to travel for continuing education, the County Administrator may be required to travel out-of-county for the following purposes:
1. Legislative Conferences, policy conferences, ICMA, FACM, and FCCMA meetings and/or other meetings scheduled by other regional or local government associations;
2. Meeting with State Representatives and/or State Agencies regarding County projects, programs and/or legislative issues;
3. Representing the County before the Legislature and State Agencies concerning County matters.
